RULE: Commit Message Summary (footer by default; git on skill call)

OBJECTIVE:
After work that creates, updates, or deletes repository files, close the response with a short change summary and a **recommended commit message**. Do **not** run `git add`, `git commit`, or `git push` on ordinary turns.

When the user explicitly invokes `/commit-message-summary` (or asks to run that skill), follow `.cursor/skills/commit-message-summary/SKILL.md` Workflow B: inventory, draft, then stage, commit, and push in one go.

DEPENDENCY:
- Detailed workflow and templates: `.cursor/skills/commit-message-summary/SKILL.md`

WHEN TO INCLUDE FOOTER:
- Implementation, fixes, refactors, styles, tests, or docs that touched the codebase
- User asked for a commit message (footer only unless they invoked the skill)

WHEN TO SKIP FOOTER:
- Question-only or review-only turns with no file changes
- User explicitly opted out of the commit footer for that turn

REQUIREMENTS:

1. SUMMARY
- 2–5 bullets: what changed and why (group by feature, not every filename)

2. COMMIT MESSAGE
- Format: `<type>: <imperative subject>` optional body for why
- Types: feat, fix, style, refactor, test, docs, chore
- Align with existing repo commit style (see recent `git log`)

3. FOOTER (last section of the response — default turns)
Use this structure:

---
## Recommended commit message

```
<message here>
```

**Changed:** <brief roll-up>

_Manual commit only — not executed by the agent. Invoke /commit-message-summary to add, commit, and push._

4. PROHIBITED ON ORDINARY TURNS
- Do not run `git commit`, `git push`, or `gh` unless the user explicitly invoked this skill or separately requested git actions
- Do not stage or commit on the user's behalf as part of the default footer rule
